        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "51ec4cfe-3e4c-5980-a275-73f36b5d0657",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Hawkins County, Sullivan County, North Carolina, Washington County, Claiborne County, Hancock County, Tennessee, Lee County, Virginia, Cumberland Gap, Kentucky, Washington, Pennsylvania, Ohio Valley, Arcadia, Tennessee, Jenkins, Kentucky, Eastern Kentucky, Southwest Virginia\n- Events: Greene–Jones War\n- Routes: Wilderness Road, National Road\n- People: Henry Clay, Daniel Boone\n- Organizations: WKVG\n- Topics: Melungeon population, Southern Gospel radio\n\nFacts:\n- In 1775, Daniel Boone's Wilderness Road ran through Arcadia, Tennessee.\n- Early in the 19th century, despite Henry Clay's advocacy for the Wilderness Road route, the northern route was selected for the National Road, connecting near Washington, Pennsylvania, into the Ohio Valley of northern Kentucky and Ohio.\n- The Greene–Jones War took place primarily in border areas of Hawkins County, Washington County, Claiborne County, and Hancock County in Tennessee, and Lee County, Virginia, near Cumberland Gap.\n- Hawkins County had formerly been part of Sullivan County, North Carolina, one of the earliest Tennessee counties and said to have had a large Melungeon population.\n- Hancock County, Tennessee, was created when Hawkins County, Tennessee, was subdivided.\n- Cumberland Gap was a narrow Appalachian Mountain pass sometimes called the Wilderness Road leading into Kentucky.\n- WKVG is a Southern Gospel formatted broadcast radio station licensed to Jenkins, Kentucky, serving Eastern Kentucky and Southwest Virginia.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "a05e1451-64e5-53e6-b985-21a52c8d19ea",
        "text": "Page: Greene–Jones War. Sentence: The feud took place primarily in the border areas of Hawkins County (formerly part of Sullivan County, North Carolina, one of the earliest Tennessee counties and said to have a large Melungeon population), Washington County, Claiborne County, Hancock County, Tennessee (created when Hawkins County, Tennessee was subdivided), and Lee County, Virginia, not far from Cumberland Gap, the narrow Appalachian Mountain pass sometimes called The Wilderness Road leading into Kentucky.\nPage: Wilderness Road. Sentence: Despite Kentucky Senator Henry Clay's advocacy of this route, early in the 19th century, the northern route was selected for the National Road, connecting near Washington, Pennsylvania into the Ohio Valley of northern Kentucky and Ohio.\nPage: Arcadia, Tennessee. Sentence: Daniel Boone's Wilderness Road ran through this community in 1775.\nPage: WKVG. Sentence: WKVG is a Southern Gospel formatted broadcast radio station licensed to Jenkins, Kentucky, serving Eastern Kentucky and Southwest Virginia.",
